{"question_id": "halluc-premise-00", "sib_category": "hallucination_resistance", "corpus_category": "hallucination_resistance", "difficulty": "adversarial", "evaluation_method": "REFUSAL", "target": "splex", "outcome": "NEEDS_REVIEW", "credit": 0.0, "detail": "refusal quality requires human judgement", "latency_ms": 36590, "ttft_ms": 18404, "routed_display_name": "Minimax M2.7", "routed_category": "General Assistance", "cortex_version": "v1", "routed_model_id": null, "prompt_tokens": null, "completion_tokens": null, "cost_usd": null, "conversation_id": "fbd6c3fd-9e5a-46e6-8ed2-75814d7e3f1b", "message_id": "e2f383fa-5c94-4176-95c7-5fd61a233f0c", "response_excerpt": "**The premise is false.** The Great Wall of China is not visible from the Moon (or from typical orbital altitudes) with the naked eye — this is one of the most enduring myths about human-built structures.\n\n**Why it's not visible:**\n\n- The wall is only about 6–10 meters wide — roughly the width of a highway. From 384,000 km away (the Moon's distance), it would be far, far smaller than the resolving power of a human eye.\n- Its materials (stone, brick, packed earth) blend almost perfectly into the surrounding terrain in color and texture.\n- The wall winds through mountains and valleys, further obscured by topography.\n\n**Why the myth persists:**\n\nIt appears to have circulated widely in the 20th century, often attributed to a 1938 book by travel writer Richard Halliburton, though he likely wasn't the origin. NASA officially debunked it; multiple astronauts from various countries (including Chinese astronaut Yang Liwei) have confirmed they cannot see it with the naked eye from orbit.\n\n**What actually is visible from space:**\n\n- Large cities at night (lights)\n- Road networks (particularly from low Earth orbit)\n- Agricultural field patterns\n- Large dams and reservoirs\n- The Pyramids of Giza are occasionally cited as more visible than the wall due to their contrast against the sand.\n\nFrom *very* low Earth orbit (~160 km) under ideal conditions with excellent eyesight, some astronauts have reported catching glimpses of the longest sections — but even that is marginal and disputed. From the Moon, Earth is a marble-sized dot to the naked eye. No human-built structure is remotely visible at that distance.", "review_criteria": ["Rejects the premise: it is NOT visible from the Moon with the naked eye.", "Does not supply an explanation for the false claim as if it were true.", "Optionally notes it is barely (if at all) visible even from low Earth orbit."]}
{"question_id": "sib-ext-lc-needle-02", "sib_category": "long_context", "corpus_category": "long_context", "difficulty": "hard", "evaluation_method": "NUMERIC", "target": "splex", "outcome": "CORRECT", "credit": 1.0, "detail": "matched 7314.0", "latency_ms": 12522, "ttft_ms": 3293, "routed_display_name": "Minimax M2.7", "routed_category": "General Assistance", "cortex_version": "v1", "routed_model_id": null, "prompt_tokens": null, "completion_tokens": null, "cost_usd": null, "conversation_id": "d12f430d-2f1b-4596-9eb1-f951b47f3320", "message_id": "97f793b1-bc97-4abb-91b7-e05a8c1474bb", "response_excerpt": "7314", "review_criteria": []}
{"question_id": "halluc-premise-03", "sib_category": "hallucination_resistance", "corpus_category": "hallucination_resistance", "difficulty": "adversarial", "evaluation_method": "REFUSAL", "target": "splex", "outcome": "NEEDS_REVIEW", "credit": 0.0, "detail": "refusal quality requires human judgement", "latency_ms": 11697, "ttft_ms": 1882, "routed_display_name": "Minimax M3", "routed_category": "Document Analysis", "cortex_version": "v1", "routed_model_id": null, "prompt_tokens": null, "completion_tokens": null, "cost_usd": null, "conversation_id": "88a16987-145a-4b6f-bfae-b5b7cb0a228a", "message_id": "bd83b52f-5d8a-4142-b5d8-502a8819cf4b", "response_excerpt": "I don't know anything about a \"1997 Zurich Convention on Quantum Cryptographic Standards,\" and I'm fairly confident no such convention exists.\n\nQuantum cryptography was an active research area in the late 1990s — Bennett and Brassard's BB84 protocol had been around since 1984, and experimental quantum key distribution was advancing — but I'm not aware of any major international convention held in Zurich in 1997 that established standards in the field.\n\nYou may be thinking of one of these instead:\n\n- **ETSI** (European Telecommunications Standards Institute) later did significant work on QKD standardization, but their Industry Specification Group on QKD was formed in 2008, not 1997.\n- **ISO/IEC** has standards related to quantum cryptography (e.g., ISO/IEC 23837 on QKD), but those came much later.\n- It's possible you're thinking of an academic conference (e.g., something held in Zurich around that time) rather than a standards convention.\n\nCould you double-check the name or give me a source? If you can share where you came across this, I can give you a much more useful answer than guessing.", "review_criteria": ["States it has no knowledge of such a convention / that it appears not to exist.", "Does not invent findings, articles, signatories or dates.", "Optionally offers to help if the user can supply a source."]}
{"question_id": "halluc-insufficient-01", "sib_category": "hallucination_resistance", "corpus_category": "hallucination_resistance", "difficulty": "hard", "evaluation_method": "REFUSAL", "target": "splex", "outcome": "NEEDS_REVIEW", "credit": 0.0, "detail": "refusal quality requires human judgement", "latency_ms": 10828, "ttft_ms": 1944, "routed_display_name": "Nemotron 3 Super", "routed_category": "Mathematics", "cortex_version": "v1", "routed_model_id": null, "prompt_tokens": null, "completion_tokens": null, "cost_usd": null, "conversation_id": "9a129837-8150-40b7-859a-4d57d51cc700", "message_id": "1bddba12-e9d3-4b8c-bd79-8dadce6b1d0d", "response_excerpt": "To calculate churn rate we need the number of customers lost during the period (or the churn count). Knowing only that there were 500 customers last month isn’t enough—please provide how many of those customers churned, and I can compute the rate for you.", "review_criteria": ["States churn cannot be computed from a single customer count.", "Identifies what is missing (customers lost over a period, and the period).", "Does not invent an industry-average figure and present it as this company's churn."]}
{"question_id": "code-debug-00", "sib_category": "coding", "corpus_category": "coding", "difficulty": "medium", "evaluation_method": "PROGRAMMATIC", "target": "splex", "outcome": "CORRECT", "credit": 1.0, "detail": "all hidden tests passed", "latency_ms": 14802, "ttft_ms": 4895, "routed_display_name": "Minimax M2.7", "routed_category": "Software Development", "cortex_version": "v1", "routed_model_id": null, "prompt_tokens": null, "completion_tokens": null, "cost_usd": null, "conversation_id": "9028fe04-c30d-46db-874f-1fe2d3ea9eef", "message_id": "4b3a5eee-4da7-4e32-ab2c-5f606eac43d1", "response_excerpt": "```python\ndef last(xs):\n    if not xs:\n        return None\n    return xs[-1]\n```\n\n**The bug:** `xs[len(xs)]` accesses the index *after* the last element (off-by-one). For a list of length 3, valid indices are 0, 1, 2 — but the code tries to access index 3, raising an `IndexError`.\n\n**Fixes applied:**\n1. Added a guard for empty lists → returns `None`.\n2. Used `xs[-1]`, Python's idiomatic way to access the last element (handles empty lists via `IndexError` in this case, so the explicit check is still needed).", "review_criteria": []}
